    {
      "name": "a",
      "reason": "contextual-keyword",
      "positions": [
        "is-predicate"
      ],
      "rationale": "Structural by position only (spec/grammar.md:380). `define a` is legal and `:p is a \"point\"` still reads as the predicate afterwards, because no declaration can occupy the position that makes the word structural."
    },
    {
      "name": "empty",
      "reason": "contextual-keyword",
      "positions": [
        "is-predicate"
      ],
      "rationale": "Structural by position only (spec/grammar.md:380). `define empty` is legal and `:x is empty` still reads as the predicate afterwards."
    },
    {
      "name": "member",
      "reason": "contextual-keyword",
      "positions": [
        "is-predicate"
      ],
      "rationale": "Structural by position only (spec/grammar.md:380). `define member` is legal and `2 is member of :nums` still reads as the predicate afterwards. Distinct from the Core reporter `member?`, which IS a built-in name."
    },
    {
      "name": "of",
      "reason": "contextual-keyword",
      "positions": [
        "is-predicate",
        "value-of-reader"
      ],
      "rationale": "Structural in two positions and a built-in name in neither (spec/grammar.md:380). `define of` is legal and `value of :d for key \"a\"` still reports the key's value afterwards. `positions` records where the grammar makes the word structural; it says nothing about how the word is painted, which is an independent axis (LDR-0007)."
    }
  ]
